Template: drumee-infra/tls_method
Type: select
Choices: acme-dns-server, acme-dns-api, caddy, own, self-signed
Default: acme-dns-server
Description: How should DNS and TLS certificates be handled?
 Drumee needs a WILDCARD certificate (example.com plus *.example.com), which
 can only be issued through a DNS-01 challenge — never over HTTP. So this
 answer decides two things at once: how that challenge is answered, and who
 runs DNS for your domain.
 .
 acme-dns-server: Drumee provides the DNS. BIND9 is installed here and becomes
 the authoritative nameserver for your domain, serving the complete zone — ns1
 and ns2, the apex and its wildcard, jit, vendors and *.vendors, the MX record,
 and the SPF, DKIM and DMARC records that let mail sent by this instance be
 accepted elsewhere. The certificate challenge is then answered locally through
 a TSIG-signed dynamic update, as one record among all those. Requires the
 domain's NS records to be delegated to this machine and inbound UDP port 53 to
 reach it — so it does NOT work behind a home router.
 .
 acme-dns-api: the challenge record is created through your DNS provider's API
 (any provider supported by acme.sh). This needs only outbound access, so it
 works behind NAT with no port forwarding at all. No DNS server is run here:
 every other record stays yours to publish at your provider, including jit,
 vendors, *.vendors and the mail authentication records — without those, mail
 from this instance is likely to be rejected as spam.
 .
 caddy: Caddy obtains and renews the certificates itself, using the DNS
 provider module it was built with, and terminates TLS on ports 80/443 in front
 of nginx (nginx moves to internal ports). Also outbound-only, so it works
 behind NAT. As with acme-dns-api, no DNS server is run here and the zone
 remains yours to publish. Requires the drumee-caddy package; without it this
 choice is refused and nginx keeps ports 80/443.
 .
 own: you already have wildcard certificates and will point Drumee at them. No
 DNS server is run here; the zone remains yours to publish.
 .
 self-signed: no public certificate — for a LAN-only or test instance. BIND9 is
 installed here and serves the zone for your domain on this machine, because
 nothing else on the network knows that name: a private domain is not delegated
 anywhere, so without a resolver here it resolves for nobody. Point the LAN at
 this host afterwards (the router's DHCP "DNS server" option, or each client's
 resolver) and the name works everywhere on it. Only meaningful together with a
 local domain; on a public domain a certificate is still requested (use "own" to
 supply your own instead).

Template: drumee-infra/acme_env_file
Type: string
Default: /etc/drumee/credential/dns-api.env
Description: Please enter the path to the DNS provider credentials file
 A shell file that will be sourced before the certificate is requested. It must
 export ACME_PROVIDER (an acme.sh dnsapi name, without the "dns_" prefix, e.g.
 ovh, cloudflare, gandi_livedns) plus the variables that provider needs.
 .
 For example, for OVH:
 .
   export ACME_PROVIDER=ovh
   export OVH_AK=... OVH_AS=... OVH_CK=...
 .
 Create the file with mode 0600 before this package is configured. If it is
 missing, Drumee falls back to running a local DNS server instead.

Template: drumee-infra/caddy_domain
Type: string
Description: Please enter the domain to certify with Caddy
 The certificate covers this domain and its subdomains (a wildcard is possible
 because Caddy validates over DNS). Defaults to this instance's domain; change
 it only if the certificate should be issued for a different zone.

Template: drumee-infra/caddy_dns_provider
Type: string
Description: Please enter the Caddy DNS provider module
 The name of the caddy-dns module the drumee-caddy binary was built with, for
 example ovh, cloudflare, gandi, desec or route53. It must match a module that
 is actually compiled in; see the drumee-caddy package documentation for the
 list it ships with.

Template: drumee-infra/caddy_dns_api_key
Type: password
Description: Please enter the DNS provider API key or token
 Used by Caddy to create the DNS challenge record. It is written to
 /etc/drumee/credential/caddy-dns.env with mode 0600 and is never echoed back.
 .
 Providers needing more than one value (an application key plus a secret, for
 instance) take the extra values in that file; enter the primary token here and
 complete the file afterwards.

Template: drumee-infra/own_ssl
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Do you have your own SSL certificates?
 Superseded by drumee-infra/tls_method; kept so existing preseeds keep working.
 Setting it true is equivalent to choosing "own".

Template: drumee-infra/own_ssl_path
Type: string
Description: Please enter the location of your SSL certificates
 Certificates must be wildcard and cover every name Drumee serves:
 example.com, *.example.com, jit.example.com, *.jit.example.com,
 vendors.example.com and *.vendors.example.com.
 .
 Each subdomain needs its own wildcard entry: a wildcard matches a single label,
 so *.example.com does NOT cover anything below vendors.example.com.
 .
 This directory must EXIST, with the certificates already in it, before the
 package is configured — otherwise Drumee ignores the setting and requests a
 certificate instead. nginx reads them by name, in the acme.sh layout, one
 subdirectory per certified name:
 .
   <this directory>/example.com_ecc/fullchain.cer   (leaf + intermediates)
   <this directory>/example.com_ecc/ca.cer          (intermediates only)
   <this directory>/example.com_ecc/example.com.key
 .
 and the same for vendors.example.com_ecc and jit.example.com_ecc. A flat
 directory of .pem files will not be found.

Template: drumee-infra/reconfigure_existing
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Re-generate the configuration of this existing Drumee instance?
 This host is already configured — /etc/drumee/drumee.json names a domain. By
 default an upgrade installs the new package but does NOT regenerate the
 configuration tree, so the instance keeps running exactly as it is.
 .
 The consequence of declining: configuration fixes shipped with this version do
 not reach this host. Corrections to the nginx vhosts, the routes, the certificate
 paths, the DNS zone or the mail configuration all arrive by re-rendering, so an
 instance that never re-renders keeps whatever those files said when it was first
 installed.
 .
 The consequence of accepting: every file rendered from your answers is rewritten
 from those answers — /etc/drumee, the nginx vhosts and includes, the BIND zones,
 Prosody and Postfix. Anything you edited by hand in those files is lost, and the
 DNS zone serial changes. Passwords are NOT regenerated: the database, mail and
 butler secrets already on this host are reused, so the instance keeps working
 against its own database.
 .
 Answer yes to adopt this version's configuration, no to leave the running
 configuration untouched. You can do it later at any time with:
 dpkg-reconfigure drumee-infra

Template: drumee-infra/wireguard_enabled
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Enable WireGuard peer coordination?
 Lets this instance be reached from outside without opening any port on the
 local router. The node keeps an outbound connection to a coordination
 server, which pairs it with clients so both sides punch their firewall at
 the same instant. Traffic stays peer-to-peer and end-to-end encrypted; a
 relay is used only when the direct path cannot be established.

Template: drumee-infra/wireguard_coordinator
Type: string
Default: coord.drumee.tech
Description: Please enter the coordination server host
 Hostname of the Drumee coordination server. It handles signaling only and
 never sees the content of your traffic.

Template: drumee-infra/wireguard_listen_port
Type: string
Default: 51820
Description: Please enter the WireGuard listen port
 UDP port used by this node. It must stay fixed: the agent learns its public
 NAT mapping by probing from this exact port, and a changing port would make
 that mapping useless.

Template: drumee-infra/wireguard_reflector_port
Type: string
Default: 51821
Description: Please enter the coordination server reflector port
 UDP port of the endpoint reflector on the coordination server.
